## Verdict

**KNOWN — literature located through 18 October 2026.** I did not find an unconditional \(L[1/3]\) factoring theorem, a proof of LV fruitfulness for almost all balanced semiprimes, or an unconditional general factoring bound \(L[1/2,c]\) with \(c<1\). This is a search result, not a theorem of nonexistence. LV’s factoring conclusion remains explicitly conditional in the paper: Theorem 2.3 assumes Conjecture 7.1. [Lee–Venkatesan, §7 and Theorem 2.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873).

**PROVED below.** There is a useful positive averaging theorem: Jacobi signs are balanced on a fixed set of smooth integers for almost all balanced Blum semiprimes, at the relevant subexponential lengths. Ordinary large sieve methods suffice, without an \(\varepsilon\)-loss. However, even these estimates for **every fixed linear test** can coexist with \(\delta=0\) for **every modulus**. Thus decoupling the smoothness weights is necessary for this approach but does not resolve the adaptive minimum over \(w\).

**PROVED below.** Several proposed symmetries have precise barriers:

- LV’s odd-degree fields admit no nonidentity field automorphism of order two.
- QS conjugation preserves the actual relation.
- A primitive degree-one pair of odd determinant has no nontrivial integral linear involution preserving the two forms individually up to signs, apart from simultaneous negation.
- An explicit square-class-preserving transformation with a fruitful two-relation orbit already yields a factor directly.

**KNOWN — relevant recent progress.** Two developments deserve attention: Pascadi’s \(5/8\) exponent of distribution for smooth numbers, and de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski’s rigorous ideal-sampling and class-group algorithms under ERH. Neither currently supplies NFS fruitfulness or the required paired, short-generator special-\(q\) descent theorem. [Pascadi, Theorem 1.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.00653), [de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski, Theorems 1.1 and 1.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.01588v2).

I use the project’s Lemma H, Bockstein formula, and Proposition R as **KNOWN — supplied background**.

