## 1. Literature

### 1.1 Factoring and fruitfulness

**KNOWN.** Lee and Venkatesan, *Rigorous analysis of a randomised number field sieve*, **J. Number Theory 187 (2018), 92–159**, prove their congruence-generation bounds. Their Theorem 2.3 obtains a nontrivial congruence for a fixed Blum semiprime only conditional on Conjecture 7.1. Section 7 also discusses the multiple-polynomial version; it does not eliminate that assumption. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873).

**KNOWN.** Lenstra and Pomerance, *A rigorous time bound for factoring integers*, **J. Amer. Math. Soc. 5 (1992), 483–516**, Theorem 10.5, give expected complete factorization time
\[
L_n[1/2,1+o(1)].
\]
Their remark following Theorem 10.3 says that Algorithm 10.1 itself has expected time \(L_n[1/2,1+o(1)]\), and changing its smoothness parameter does not improve that constant. This is a limitation of that algorithm, not a lower bound for factoring. [Author’s copy](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf).

**KNOWN — search outcome.** I found no 2018–2026 paper proving any of the following:

1. unconditional LV/NFS fruitfulness for all inputs;
2. such fruitfulness for almost all balanced semiprimes or Blum semiprimes;
3. an unconditional general factoring bound \(L[1/2,c]\), \(c<1\);
4. an unconditional fixed-degree NFS factoring analysis achieving such a constant.

**KNOWN — verification limitation.** A CANARI bibliography lists Razvan Barbulescu’s *Rigorous time bound for factoring with elliptic curves*, HAL-03485435. I could verify the listing but could not access a theorem statement or manuscript through HAL’s access barrier. I therefore do **not** treat that title as evidence of an improved factoring bound. [Listing](https://canari.math.u-bordeaux.fr/publications/2026.html).

**PROVED — qualification concerning “any family.”** Elementary special families do have better bounds. If \(n=uv\), with \(u,v\) odd and \(u\le v\), Fermat’s method takes at most
\[
1+\frac{(v-u)^2}{8\sqrt n}
\]
iterations, up to polynomial factors in \(\log n\).

Indeed, the successful value is \(a=(u+v)/2\), and
\[
a-\sqrt n
=\frac{(v-u)^2}{2(u+v+2\sqrt n)}
\le \frac{(v-u)^2}{8\sqrt n}.
\]
Thus sufficiently close-factor families have \(L[1/3]\), or smaller, factoring time. These do not establish fruitfulness of a sieve or an almost-all theorem for balanced semiprimes.

### 1.2 Descent, fixed degree, and polynomial smoothness

**KNOWN.** Haetham Al Aswad and Cécile Pierrot, *Individual Discrete Logarithm with Sublattice Reduction*, arXiv:2309.13048, give rigorous lattice and norm bounds, but their conversion into smoothness-search complexity is conditional. In particular, Theorem 5.2 explicitly assumes that the output norm is uniformly distributed over the relevant integer interval. Under that assumption, a norm bound \(Q^e\) leads to the stated initial-splitting complexity \(L_Q[1/3,(3e)^{1/3}]\). It is not an unconditional special-\(q\) smoothness theorem. [§5.3, Theorem 5.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2309.13048).

**KNOWN.** Barbulescu and Jouve, *ECM and the Elliott–Halberstam conjecture for quadratic fields*, **Acta Arith. 213 (2024), 289–342**, prove conditional smoothness results relevant to NFS initial splitting. Their Theorem 1.2 gives, for a CM elliptic curve and bounded \(u=\log x/\log y\), the asymptotic
\[
\psi_E(x,y)\sim \rho(u)\frac{x}{\log x}
\]
assuming the stated quadratic-field Elliott–Halberstam conjecture. The \(L[1/3]\) conclusion in Theorem 2.1(2) requires a further extension to a substantially larger growing-\(u,v\) range. This is neither unconditional NFS descent nor an unconditional \(L[1/3]\) splitting analysis. [Theorems 1.2, 1.3 and 2.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.11724).

**KNOWN.** De Boer, Pellet-Mary and Wesolowski, *Rigorous methods for computational number theory*, arXiv:2512.01588v2, 19 February 2026, provide a genuine new rigorous tool under ERH:

- Theorem 1.1 samples \(\beta\in\mathfrak a\) with
  \[
  (\beta)\mathfrak a^{-1}\in\mathcal S\mathcal S_B
  \]
  with probability at least \(\delta_{\mathcal S}[r^d]/3-\varepsilon\), subject to their explicit size condition on \(r\); here \(B\) is polylogarithmic in the discriminant and accuracy parameters.
- Theorem 1.3 computes class groups and compact fundamental units in time polynomial in input length,
  \[
  L_{|\Delta_K|}[1/2,O(1)],\quad
  L_{d^d}[2/3,O(1)],\quad
  \min\!\left(\rho_K,L_{|\Delta_K|}[2/3+o(1),O(1)]\right).
  \]

They explicitly leave NFS applications uninvestigated. [Theorems 1.1, 1.3; introduction, “Further applications”](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.01588v2).

**PROVED — scope distinction.** Ideal decomposition in a number field does not by itself provide NFS special-\(q\) descent. The latter additionally requires appropriately short generators in a prescribed relation shape, compatibility with the finite-field evaluation, and suitable smoothness of the other channel. A theorem about arbitrary ideal-lattice samples does not imply a theorem about the two-dimensional slice \(a-b\theta\).

**KNOWN.** Relevant polynomial-smoothness results include:

- Balog–Blomer–Dartyge–Tenenbaum, *Friable values of binary forms*, **Comment. Math. Helv. 87 (2012), 639–667**: for a fixed degree-\(g\) form, their general positive-proportion bound uses \(y=x^{g-2+\varepsilon}\); for cubic forms they obtain \(y=x^{1/\sqrt e+\varepsilon}\). These are fixed-form, power-smoothness results. [Paper](https://tenenb.perso.math.cnrs.fr/PPP/friableforms.pdf).
- Bober–Fretwell–Martin–Wooley, *Smooth values of polynomials*, **J. Aust. Math. Soc. 108 (2020), 245–261**, Theorem 1.1: for a quadratic \(f\), there are auxiliary polynomials \(g\) of arbitrarily large odd degree \(k\) such that every factor of \(f(g(t))\) has degree at most \(ck/\sqrt{\log\log k}\). Consequently, for each \(\varepsilon>0\), infinitely many \(n\) satisfy \(P^+(f(n))\le n^\varepsilon\). This supplies no density in the required QS interval. [Theorem 1.1](https://www.math.purdue.edu/~twooley/publ/20171004smoothpoly.pdf).
- Mine, *An upper bound for the number of smooth values of a polynomial and its applications*, arXiv:2410.09558: an upper-bound theorem, not the joint lower bound needed here. [Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2410.09558).

**KNOWN — search outcome.** I did not find a theorem giving the needed uniform, joint smoothness lower bound for random NFS polynomials in adaptively chosen special-\(q\) sublattices, nor a published fixed-degree \(L[1/2]\) NFS factoring analysis that avoids a fruitfulness assumption.

### 1.3 Modern smooth-number distribution

**KNOWN.** Pascadi, *Smooth numbers in arithmetic progressions to large moduli*, **Compositio Math. 161 (2025), 1923–1974**, obtains exponent \(66/107\). His subsequent preprint *On the exponents of distribution of primes and smooth numbers*, arXiv:2505.00653, Theorem 1.5, gives the following stronger statement.

For fixed \(a\ne0\), \(A,\varepsilon>0\), there is \(C=C(a,A,\varepsilon)\) such that, for
\[
(\log x)^C\le y\le x^{1/C},\qquad Q\le x^{5/8-\varepsilon},
\]
\[
\sum_{\substack{q\le Q\\(q,a)=1}}
\left|
\Psi(x,y;a,q)-\frac{\Psi_q(x,y)}{\varphi(q)}
\right|
\ll_{\varepsilon,A,a}\frac{\Psi(x,y)}{(\log x)^A}.
\]
The fixed/small-residue restriction matters. [2025 published paper](https://doi.org/10.1112/S0010437X2500747X), [subsequent Theorem 1.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.00653).

**PROVED — application limitation.** This statement cannot simply be substituted into LV with an arbitrary residue depending on \(n,m,a,b,f\): its constant and hypotheses concern a fixed residue, whereas LV needs control after conditioning the modulus to be smooth and allowing the relevant residue to vary. Such a substitution requires a separate argument.

**KNOWN.** Jain, *Smooth Numbers in Short Intervals*, arXiv:2502.10530v1, Theorem 1.1, proves existence in almost all intervals of length
\[
h\ge
\exp\!\left((1+\varepsilon)
\left(\frac{11}{8}u\log u+4\log\log x\right)\right)
\]
when
\[
\exp\!\left(C(\log x)^{2/3}(\log\log x)^{4/3}\right)
\le y\le x^{1/C}.
\]
This is outside the smaller smoothness ranges relevant to the proposed LP improvement and the present NFS application. [Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.10530).

**KNOWN.** Matomäki–Radziwiłł, *Multiplicative functions in short intervals*, **Ann. Math. 183 (2016), 1015–1056**, Theorem 1, compares short and long averages of real multiplicative functions bounded by one; the result is uniform in the function. Their later *Multiplicative functions in short intervals II* develops results for sparse multiplicative sequences. [2016 paper](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/annals-v183-n3-p06-p.pdf), [later paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2007.04290).

**PROVED — application limitation.** Applying such a comparison to a smooth-supported character still requires a suitable long signed average and error smaller than the smooth-set density. Furthermore, a short interval in the integer variable is different from values along \(a^2-n\), or a paired norm form in a prescribed lattice. These logical requirements remain even when a relative short-interval estimate is available.

### 1.4 Heath-Brown’s quadratic large sieve and loss-free alternatives

**KNOWN.** Heath-Brown, *A mean value estimate for real character sums*, **Acta Arith. 72 (1995), 235–275**, Theorem 1, states
\[
\sum_{\substack{m\le Q\\m\ {\rm odd,\ squarefree}}}
\left|
\sum_{\substack{t\le H\\t\ {\rm odd,\ squarefree}}}
a_t\left(\frac tm\right)
\right|^2
\ll_\varepsilon
(QH)^\varepsilon(Q+H)
\sum_t|a_t|^2.
\tag{1}
\]
Both squarefree restrictions are material. Corollary 2, for all primitive real characters of conductor at most \(Q\), replaces the final norm for unrestricted \(t\) by
\[
\mathcal E_\square(a)
=
\sum_{\substack{r,s\le H\\rs\ {\rm square}}}|a_ra_s|.
\tag{2}
\]
[Original statement, author’s copy](https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3Ab188b365-d8d1-4267-8548-49f01e6cb6a7/files/m6af40476f1b860392271a2d8ecae593b).

**KNOWN.** Zihao Liu, *Explicit quadratic large sieve inequality*, arXiv:2505.09637v2, Theorem 1, makes the constant explicit:
\[
\mathcal B(Q,H)
\le
\exp_4(C/\varepsilon)(QH)^\varepsilon(Q+H).
\tag{3}
\]
Optimizing this supplies a loss
\[
\exp\!\left(O\!\left(\frac{\log(QH)}{\log_4(QH)}\right)\right).
\tag{4}
\]
I found no general unconditional replacement by a fixed power of \(\log(QH)\) for arbitrary coefficients. Liu explicitly identifies an improvement of the \(\varepsilon\)-dependence as a further problem. [Theorem 1 and following discussion](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.09637v2).

**PROVED — ordinary large sieve alternative.** With the squarefree restrictions of (1),
\[
\boxed{\quad
\sum_m^*
\left|\sum_t^*a_t(t/m)\right|^2
\ll
\min(Q^2+H,\;Q+H^2)\sum_t^*|a_t|^2 .
\quad}
\tag{5}
\]
The implied constant is absolute; there is no logarithmic or \(\varepsilon\)-loss.

For \(Q^2+H\), include these primitive quadratic characters among all primitive characters of conductor at most \(Q\), and apply the classical multiplicative large sieve.

For \(Q+H^2\), split both variables into residue classes modulo \(4\). Quadratic reciprocity changes \((t/m)\) to a fixed sign times \((m/t)\) on each block. By duality, apply the ordinary character large sieve with character conductors at most \(H\) and coefficient length \(Q\). Combining the four blocks costs an absolute constant. The ordinary inequality is also recorded as equation (3) in Heath-Brown’s paper.

Consequently:

| Range | Loss-free consequence |
|---|---|
| \(Q\ge H^2\) | \(O(Q)\sum|a_t|^2\) |
| \(H\ge Q^2\) | \(O(H)\sum|a_t|^2\) |
| General \(Q,H\) | \(O(\min(Q^2+H,Q+H^2))\sum|a_t|^2\) |

**PROVED — interpretation.** Here \(Q\) bounds the moduli; it is not merely their number. Restricting to a sparse family of moduli preserves the upper bound but does not replace \(Q\) by the family’s cardinality.

**PROVED — scale warning.** If \(Q\asymp N\) and \(H=N^{o(1)}\), a fixed loss \(N^\varepsilon\) is larger than every fixed \(L_N[1/3,c]\). Liu’s explicit loss (4) is also much larger than that scale. One cannot declare either loss harmless in this program. In this particular unbalanced range, (5) removes the problem entirely.

