## 3. \(\beta\)-flipping involutions

### 3.1 Galois automorphisms

**PROVED — odd-degree barrier.** Let \(K/\mathbb Q\) have odd degree. Then \(K\) has no nonidentity automorphism \(\sigma\) satisfying \(\sigma^2=1\).

If such a \(\sigma\) existed, its fixed field \(F\) would satisfy \([K:F]=2\). Indeed, each \(z\in K\) satisfies
\[
T^2-(z+\sigma z)T+z\sigma z\in F[T],
\]
and the standard fixed-field argument gives degree two. This contradicts
\[
[K:\mathbb Q]=[K:F][F:\mathbb Q]
\]
being odd.

LV explicitly chooses odd degree. Thus a fixed nonidentity field automorphism cannot itself provide the requested involution.

**PROVED — relation-level version.** If \(b\ne0\), \(a-b\theta\) generates \(K\). If
\[
\sigma^2(a-b\theta)=a-b\theta,
\]
then \(\sigma^2\theta=\theta\), so \(\sigma^2=1\). Restricting such an automorphism to the linear-relation set does not evade the odd-degree barrier.

**PROVED — scope.** This does not exclude a piecewise matching of elements in longer Galois orbits, or a correspondence between different fields.

**PROVED — coordinate obstruction.** An automorphism generally permutes prime-ideal coordinates:
\[
x(\sigma r)=P_\sigma x(r).
\]
Equal ideal norms preserve smoothness, but do not establish equality of the **labelled exact** square-class vector.

**PROVED — caution about equivariance.** If evaluation commutes with an automorphism, transforming an entire true dependency preserves its fruitfulness. That observation alone does **not** rule out a fruitful two-element orbit. A pointwise involution must be checked on the pair \(\{r,Tr\}\), not only on globally transformed dependencies.

### 3.2 QS conjugation

**PROVED.** In the norm presentation
\[
\alpha=a+b\sqrt n,\qquad
N(\alpha)=a^2-nb^2,
\]
conjugation sends \(b\) to \(-b\). It preserves both the smooth integer \(a^2-nb^2\) and the modular root \(a\).

Thus it preserves the actual QS congruence relation, not just smoothness. The two-element dependency formed from a relation and its conjugate has identical modular roots and is unfruitful.

Simultaneous negation replaces the modular root by its global negative and likewise gives no nontrivial element of \(\mu_2/\{\pm1\}\).

### 3.3 Units and \(S\)-units: explicit square twists

**PROVED — general square-twist identity.** Consider relations with rational and algebraic entries \(q,\alpha\), satisfying
\[
q\equiv\varphi(\alpha)\pmod n.
\]
Suppose a transformation has explicitly known
\[
q'=s^2q,\qquad \alpha'=t^2\alpha,
\]
with \(s\in\mathbb Q^\times\), \(t\in K^\times\), and all evaluations defined and invertible modulo \(n\).

For the two-relation dependency, compatible roots are
\[
sq,\qquad t\alpha.
\]
Its root ratio is
\[
\boxed{\quad \frac{s}{\varphi(t)}\in\mu_2(\mathbb Z/n).\quad}
\tag{15}
\]
The congruence for the transformed relation proves that its square is \(1\).

If (15) is not a global sign, then
\[
\gcd\!\left(s-\varphi(t),n\right)
\]
after clearing invertible denominators gives a proper factor.

**PROVED — consequence.** A unit or \(S\)-unit twist preserving the **exact** square classes must have square quotients in the relevant fields. If their roots are explicitly available, a \(\beta\)-flipping pair already contains an immediately extractable factor.

This is a reduction, not an impossibility theorem: discovering such twists could be a factoring algorithm.

**PROVED.** A nonsquare unit can preserve ideal-valuation parities while changing the actual field square class. It therefore does not preserve the \(x\) required in this question. Omitting unit information would create a false symmetry.

### 3.4 Integral linear actions on degree-one pairs

**PROVED — rigidity theorem.** Let
\[
A=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix},
\qquad \det A=n>1
\]
with \(n\) odd, and suppose both rows are primitive. If \(M\in GL_2(\mathbb Z)\) satisfies
\[
L_1(Mz)=\epsilon_1L_1(z),\qquad
L_2(Mz)=\epsilon_2L_2(z),
\qquad \epsilon_j\in\{\pm1\},
\]
then \(M=I\) or \(M=-I\).

If the signs agree, this follows from \(AM=\pm A\). Otherwise, after a global sign,
\[
M=A^{-1}
\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}A
=
\frac1n
\begin{pmatrix}
ad+bc&2bd\\
-2ac&-ad-bc
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Integrality and oddness imply
\[
n\mid ac,\ bd,\ ad+bc.
\]
Together with \(ad-bc=n\), they imply \(n\mid ad,bc\). But
\[
\gcd(ac,ad,bc,bd)
=\gcd(a,b)\gcd(c,d)=1,
\]
a contradiction.

**PROVED.** Exchanging the forms generally exchanges their coordinate blocks rather than preserving \(x\). In the supplied degree-one Bockstein formula, an exact exchange preserves \(\Omega_T(L_1L_2)\) and hence does not flip \(\beta\).

**PROVED — scope.** This theorem does not exclude a nonlinear map defined only on selected smooth points, or a transformation preserving square classes without preserving the individual forms up to signs.

### 3.5 Multiple polynomials and two polynomial pairs

**KNOWN.** Coppersmith, *Modifications to the Number Field Sieve*, **J. Cryptology 6 (1993), 169–180**, reuses smoothness computations across number fields to improve the heuristic asymptotic constant. LV also analyzes a randomized multiple-polynomial variant. Neither result provides a \(\beta\)-flipping correspondence. [Coppersmith’s paper description](https://research.ibm.com/publications/modifications-to-the-number-field-sieve), [LV, §7](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873).

**PROVED.** Having the same root modulo \(n\) does not identify the fields’ square-class groups or their square-root choices. The combined relation vector has separate algebraic coordinate blocks. A shared \((a,b)\) does not establish equal exact \(x\).

**SPECULATIVE.** A productive construction would be a correspondence between two polynomial pairs supplying:

1. common smooth relations;
2. an explicit identification of the exact square-class data;
3. a proven opposite Bockstein label;
4. adequate mass under the search law.

I found no example meeting all four conditions. The first condition alone is substantially weaker.

---

## 4. Fruitfulness-free routes

### 4.1 Transplanting relation generation to class groups

**KNOWN.** The Lenstra–Pomerance method already obtains its nontriviality from class-group and ambiguous-form structure rather than an NFS fruitfulness conjecture. Its small multipliers remove the analytic assumptions needed for the relation-supply argument. They are bounded multipliers, not a replacement of discriminant size \(n\) by a subexponential discriminant.

**PROVED — optimization in the usual cost model.** Set
\[
B=L_n[1/2,b].
\]
For random integers of size \(n^{1/2+o(1)}\), the smoothness density is
\[
L_n[1/2,-1/(4b)+o(1)].
\]
If approximately \(B\) relations are required, smoothness testing has \(B^{o(1)}\) cost, and sparse linear algebra costs \(B^{2+o(1)}\), the resulting constant is
\[
c(b)=\max\left(b+\frac1{4b},\,2b\right).
\tag{16}
\]
Since \(b+1/(4b)\ge1\), the minimum is \(1\), attained at \(b=1/2\).

This calculation is conditional on that sampling model. It is not a lower bound for all class-group algorithms. It agrees with the specific LP limitation cited above.

#### A geometric obstruction to reducing the norms

**PROVED.** Let \(K\) be imaginary quadratic with fundamental discriminant \(\Delta=-D\). Every nonrational algebraic integer can be written
\[
\alpha=\frac{u+v\sqrt{-D}}2,\qquad v\ne0,
\]
with suitable integral parity conditions. Therefore
\[
|N_{K/\mathbb Q}(\alpha)|
=\frac{u^2+Dv^2}{4}\ge\frac D4.
\tag{17}
\]
Consequently, if \(N\mathfrak a\le D^{1/2+o(1)}\),
\[
N\big((\alpha)\mathfrak a^{-1}\big)
\ge D^{1/2-o(1)}/4
\]
for a nonrational \(\alpha\in\mathfrak a\).

Thus the small norms of an unrelated high-degree NFS field cannot simply be imported into the imaginary quadratic field of discriminant \(-kn\).

#### A counting obstruction for nearly uniform class sampling

**PROVED.** The number of integral ideals of norm at most \(Y\) in an imaginary quadratic field is at most
\[
\sum_{m\le Y}d(m)\le Y(1+\log Y).
\tag{18}
\]
At each rational prime, the number of ideals of norm \(p^k\) is at most \(k+1\); multiplicativity gives the first inequality. The second follows from
\[
\sum_{m\le Y}d(m)
=\sum_{r\le Y}\lfloor Y/r\rfloor.
\]

Hence, if a class sampler assigns probability at most \(A/h(\Delta)\) to each class,
\[
\Pr(\text{sampled class has an integral representative of norm }\le Y)
\le \frac{A\,Y(1+\log Y)}{h(\Delta)}.
\tag{19}
\]

**KNOWN.** Siegel’s theorem gives
\[
h(-D)=D^{1/2+o(1)}
\]
for fundamental imaginary quadratic discriminants; the lower estimate is ineffective. [Siegel, *Über die Classenzahl quadratischer Zahlkörper*, Acta Arith. 1 (1935), 83–86](https://www.impan.pl/pl/wydawnictwa/czasopisma-i-serie-wydawnicze/acta-arithmetica/all/1/1/93175/uber-die-classenzahl-quadratischer-zahlkorper).

**PROVED.** Taking \(A=D^{o(1)}\) and \(Y=D^\alpha\), \(\alpha<1/2\), in (19) gives
\[
\Pr(\text{such a small representative exists})
\le D^{\alpha-1/2+o(1)}.
\tag{20}
\]
A method retaining nearly uniform class sampling cannot obtain a polynomial reduction in representative norm at only subexponential rejection cost.

**PROVED — implications for modern smoothness inputs.** Better equidistribution can improve the justification and uniformity of relation sampling. It does not alter the optimization (16) while the sample size exponent remains \(1/2\) and the random-integer smoothness law remains the benchmark. Obtaining \(c<1\) requires a change in that model: smaller effective norms, an exponentially biased supply of smooth representatives, fewer required relations, or a different group computation.

**SPECULATIVE.** The 2026 ideal-sampling theorem is worth investigating for rigorously replacing particular sampling heuristics. I do not see a derivation of \(c<1\) from its stated bounds, or a transfer yielding \(L[1/3]\) ambiguous-form computation.

### 4.2 Hidden-root injection

#### The symmetry works exactly

**PROVED — fibre-uniformity theorem.** Let \(n\) be odd with \(k\ge2\) distinct prime factors. Suppose a relation generator produces
\[
r_i^2\equiv s_i\pmod n,\qquad (r_i,n)=1,
\]
and, conditional on all public relation data, the \(r_i\) are independent and uniform in their square-root fibres. Suppose a nonempty dependency \(S\) is selected using only those public data, and \(\prod_{i\in S}s_i=y^2\) as an integer.

Then
\[
\Pr\!\left(\prod_{i\in S}r_i\not\equiv\pm y\pmod n\right)
=1-2^{1-k}.
\tag{21}
\]

Each fibre is a torsor for
\[
G=\mu_2(\mathbb Z/n),\qquad |G|=2^k.
\]
After fixing all roots except one selected root, varying that root multiplies the final quotient by a uniform element of \(G\). The quotient is therefore uniform in \(G\), and precisely two elements are global signs.

For semiprimes, the probability is \(1/2\).

**PROVED — essential conditioning.** Marginally uniform roots do not suffice if the keep rule or dependency selection exposes or depends on the hidden fibre element.

#### Forward sampling has the wrong cost

**PROVED.** For uniform \(r\in(\mathbb Z/n)^\times\), every residue has at most \(2^k\) square roots. Therefore
\[
\Pr(r^2\bmod n\text{ is }B\text{-smooth})
\le \frac{2^k\Psi(n,B)}{\varphi(n)}.
\tag{22}
\]
For balanced semiprimes and \(B=L_n[1/3,b]\), the standard smooth-number estimate gives
\[
\frac{\Psi(n,B)}n
=
L_n[2/3,-2/(3b)+o(1)].
\]
Thus this method needs at least
\[
L_n[2/3,2/(3b)+o(1)]
\tag{23}
\]
trials in expectation. This lower bound already excludes NFS-sized cost.

The smooth-number estimate used here is the classical logarithmic asymptotic associated with Canfield–Erdős–Pomerance, *On a problem of Oppenheim concerning “factorisatio numerorum”*, **J. Number Theory 17 (1983), 1–28**. [Author’s publication list, paper 39](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/).

#### Resampling an existing fibre is already factoring

**PROVED.** Given a known root \(r\), any procedure producing \(r'\) with
\[
r'^2\equiv r^2\pmod n,\qquad r'\not\equiv\pm r\pmod n
\]
immediately factors \(n\) by \(\gcd(r-r',n)\).

Therefore “first obtain an NFS relation, then randomize its root within the fibre” cannot be justified by an elementary root-randomization operation.

**PROVED — small-ratio rejection barrier.** Let \(r\) be uniform in the unit group and \(\lambda=r^2\). The probability that
\[
\lambda\equiv c/d\pmod n,\qquad
0<|c|,|d|\le H,\quad (d,n)=1,
\]
is at most
\[
\frac{4H^2\,2^k}{\varphi(n)}.
\tag{24}
\]
There are at most \(4H^2\) numerator-denominator pairs, and each represented \(\lambda\) has at most \(2^k\) preimages \(r\).

For balanced semiprimes and \(H=n^{o(1)}\), this is \(n^{-1+o(1)}\). Repeating \(n^{o(1)}\) independent attempts still has negligible success probability.

**SPECULATIVE — remaining possibility.** An efficient generator could choose its own smooth target and produce a fibre-uniform root without accepting a prescribed target. The preceding reductions do not rule that out. I found no construction achieving this at NFS cost. Establishing the required conditional uniformity is the unresolved step.

---

## 5. Best provable statement: useful averaging, with an exact limitation

The strongest conclusion I can prove here is an averaging theorem together with a counterexample to the proposed inference from fixed-test averaging to Lemma M.

### Theorem A — fixed smooth sets over balanced Blum semiprimes

**PROVED.** Choose a balanced Blum-semiprime family \(\mathcal F_N\subset[N,2N]\) of cardinality \(\asymp N/(\log N)^2\). For example, take
\[
p\in[\sqrt N,1.1\sqrt N],\qquad
q\in[1.2\sqrt N,1.3\sqrt N],
\qquad p,q\equiv3\pmod4,
\]
and \(n=pq\).

Let \(H<\sqrt N\), and let \(a_t\ge0\) be supported on odd \(t\le H\), independently of \(n\). Define
\[
A=\sum_ta_t,\qquad
E=\sum_{rs\ {\rm square}}a_ra_s.
\]
Then (6) holds.

For fixed \(a,b>0\), let
\[
H=L_N[2/3,a],\qquad B=L_N[1/3,b].
\]
For all but \(o(|\mathcal F_N|)\) inputs,
\[
\#\{t\le H:t\text{ odd and }B\text{-smooth},\ (t/n)=\pm1\}
=
\left(\frac12+o(1)\right)
\#\{t\le H:t\text{ odd and }B\text{-smooth}\}.
\tag{25}
\]

**Proof.** Write uniquely \(t=su^2\), with \(s\) odd and squarefree, and set
\[
b_s=\sum_{u:\,su^2\le H}a_{su^2}.
\]
All \(t\le H\) are coprime to every \(n\in\mathcal F_N\), so
\[
\sum_ta_t(t/n)=\sum_s b_s(s/n).
\]
Furthermore,
\[
\sum_s b_s^2=E.
\]
By the loss-free bound (5),
\[
\sum_{n\in\mathcal F_N}
\left|\sum_ta_t(t/n)\right|^2
\ll (N+H^2)E.
\]
Chebyshev’s inequality and \(|\mathcal F_N|\asymp N/(\log N)^2\) prove (6).

The stated cardinality of \(\mathcal F_N\) follows by applying the prime number theorem in the fixed progression \(3\bmod4\) to the two disjoint prime intervals. The needed statement is
\[
\pi(x;4,3)\sim \frac{x}{2\log x}.
\]
[Prime number theorem for arithmetic progressions, Theorem 3.1](https://w3.impa.br/~goncalves/NotesDavenport.pdf).

Now use \(a_t=1\) on the odd \(B\)-smooth integers up to \(H\). Each square-class fibre has at most \(\sqrt H\) elements, whence
\[
E=\sum_s b_s^2\le \sqrt H\sum_s b_s=\sqrt H\,A.
\tag{26}
\]
The standard smooth-number asymptotic gives
\[
\log(A/H)
=
-\left(\frac{a}{3b}+o(1)\right)
(\log N)^{1/3}(\log\log N)^{2/3}.
\tag{27}
\]
For completeness, the coefficient follows from
\[
u=\frac{\log H}{\log B}
=\frac ab\left(\frac{\log N}{\log\log N}\right)^{1/3},
\qquad \log(A/H)=-(1+o(1))u\log u.
\]
Restricting to odd integers preserves this logarithmic asymptotic: every smooth integer is \(2^j\) times an odd smooth integer, so the total count is at most \(1+\log_2H\) times the odd count.

Take \(\eta=1/\log N\) in (6). Since \(H^2=o(N)\), (26) bounds the exceptional proportion by
\[
\ll \frac{(\log N)^4\sqrt H}{A}=o(1)
\]
using (27). On the remaining inputs, the signed sum is \(o(A)\), proving (25). \(\square\)

### Theorem B — even perfect decoupling does not control the adaptive test

**PROVED.** There are arithmetic laws with:

- smoothness weights independent of \(n\);
- \(C=L_N[1/3,c+o(1)]\);
- almost-all cancellation for every fixed test \(w\);

but
\[
\min_w\Pr(\beta\ne\langle w,x\rangle)=0
\]
for every \(n\).

**Proof.** Fix
\[
B=L_N[1/3,c],
\]
and sample \(t\) uniformly from the odd primes at most \(B\). Use coordinates indexed by those primes, together with the required constant parity coordinate:
\[
x(t)=(e_t,1).
\]
Define
\[
\beta_n(t)=\frac{1-(t/n)}2.
\]
For each \(n\), choose the prime coordinates of \(w_n\) to be
\[
(w_n)_\ell=\frac{1-(\ell/n)}2
\]
and its parity coordinate to be zero. Then
\[
\beta_n(t)=\langle w_n,x(t)\rangle
\]
identically. Thus \(\delta_n=0\) for every modulus.

On the other hand, for any fixed \(w\),
\[
(-1)^{\beta_n(t)+\langle w,x(t)\rangle}
=(t/n)(-1)^{\langle w,x(t)\rangle}.
\]
The signed coefficients on the right are independent of \(n\). Applying (5) and Chebyshev gives, for each fixed \(w\), an exceptional proportion
\[
\ll \frac{(\log N)^2}{\eta^2\pi(B)}
=o(1)
\]
for any fixed \(\eta>0\).

Hence every fixed predictor is approximately wrong half the time for almost all moduli, while every modulus has its own perfect predictor.

The dimension is
\[
C=1+\pi(B)=L_N[1/3,c+o(1)].
\]
This proves the theorem. \(\square\)

**PROVED — exact interpretation.** Theorem B is not a counterexample to fruitfulness of actual QS or NFS relations. It is a counterexample to deducing Lemma M from these fixed-test character estimates alone. It uses the same quadratic characters, the relevant dimension, and completely decoupled smoothness weights.

### A precise sufficient hypothesis without a union over tests

**PROVED.** For any kept law, put
\[
\mu_{x,b}=\Pr(x_i=x,\beta_i=b),\qquad
\eta_*=\sum_x\min(\mu_{x,0},\mu_{x,1}).
\tag{28}
\]
Then
\[
\delta\ge\eta_*.
\tag{29}
\]
Indeed, at every \(x\), a deterministic predictor must disagree with at least the smaller of the two label masses.

Proposition R consequently gives
\[
\Pr(\Phi\equiv0\text{ on the batch kernel})
\le \exp(C\log2-M\eta_*).
\tag{30}
\]
Thus
\[
M\eta_*\ge C\log2+\lambda
\]
makes the failure probability at most \(e^{-\lambda}\).

**PROVED — precise conditional lemma for the program.** A sufficient hypothesis is:

> Under the actual LV search distribution, a set of parameters of mass \(L_N[1/3,-o(1)]\) simultaneously supports the required relation supply and satisfies
> \[
> \sum_x\min\!\left(
> \Pr(x_i=x,\beta_i=0\mid\text{kept}),
> \Pr(x_i=x,\beta_i=1\mid\text{kept})
> \right)
> \ge L_N[1/3,-o(1)].
> \]

The relation-supply and label-mixing conditions must hold **jointly** under the search law. Separate first-moment statements do not establish their intersection.

**PROVED — strength of this hypothesis.** This condition is stronger than Lemma M: it requires opposite labels inside identical \(x\)-fibres. A nonlinear deterministic function \(\beta=\beta(x)\) can have \(\delta>0\) but \(\eta_*=0\). Its advantage is that it removes all predictor quantification and is directly suited to an involution or coupling argument.

**SPECULATIVE — research assessment.** I do not currently see known analytic tools proving this fibre-mixing hypothesis for LV, QS, or degree-one NFS at the required cost. The proved averaging theorem identifies a tractable component; Theorem B identifies the additional structure that component cannot supply. The odd-degree automorphism barrier and the quadratic class-group norm barriers narrow several natural routes, but do not rule out nonlinear correspondences, structured adaptive-predictor arguments, or a new fibre-uniform relation generator.
